WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.975
This paper studies a puzzling training instability
in behavior cloning.

00:00:05.055 --> 00:00:09.787
When you train a policy network with minibatch
stochastic gradient descent,

00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:14.278
the long-horizon reward can swing wildly from one
iterate to the next,

00:00:14.358 --> 00:00:17.550
even though the behavior cloning loss barely moves.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.233
The authors trace this to what they call gradient
variance amplification,

00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:27.879
where tiny stochastic gradient noise gets amplified
into catastrophic error accumulation

00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:31.921
through the closed loop between the policy and its
environment.

00:00:32.001 --> 00:00:33.974
Most standard fixes do not help,

00:00:34.054 --> 00:00:39.941
but taking an exponential moving average of the
weights turns out to be remarkably effective,

00:00:40.021 --> 00:00:43.790
and the same story holds for autoregressive
language models.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.490
Imagine training a robot controller by imitation.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:51.999
You watch the training loss, and it drops smoothly
and stays low.

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:53.526
Everything looks fine.

00:00:53.606 --> 00:00:58.244
But if you actually deploy the policy and measure
how well it walks,

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:02.961
the score jumps around violently from one training
step to the next.

00:01:03.041 --> 00:01:04.973
This paper is about that gap.

00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:08.442
The single-step imitation loss is calm and stable,

00:01:08.522 --> 00:01:14.201
yet the thing you truly care about, the
long-horizon reward, is oscillating wildly.

00:01:14.281 --> 00:01:19.266
And because it is expensive to roll out and
evaluate at every checkpoint,

00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:21.694
you usually never see these swings,

00:01:21.774 --> 00:01:26.620
which means whichever checkpoint you happen to grab
could be a bad one.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.121
Modern deep learning is full of feedback loops.

00:01:30.201 --> 00:01:35.094
A language model's next token depends on the tokens
it already generated.

00:01:35.174 --> 00:01:39.317
A robot's next observation depends on the action it
just took.

00:01:39.397 --> 00:01:44.834
But when we train these systems, we almost always
optimize a surrogate objective,

00:01:44.914 --> 00:01:49.806
like next-token prediction, that pretends the
feedback loop is not there.

00:01:49.886 --> 00:01:52.599
That surrogate tends to improve smoothly,

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.277
which lulls us into thinking training is well behaved.

00:01:56.357 --> 00:01:59.479
The authors argue this is a dangerous illusion,

00:01:59.559 --> 00:02:04.792
and that the real culprit behind the instability is
not the data or the model,

00:02:04.872 --> 00:02:07.380
but the noise in the optimizer itself.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.461
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:11.541 --> 00:02:15.243
First,

00:02:15.323 --> 00:02:19.024
through an extensive study of two hundred and
seventy-eight distinct interventions,

00:02:19.104 --> 00:02:22.806
it diagnoses these reward oscillations and names
the mechanism gradient variance amplification,

00:02:22.886 --> 00:02:26.587
or G V A,

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:30.369
the propagation of minibatch stochastic gradient
noise through the closed-loop dynamics.

00:02:30.449 --> 00:02:34.150
Second, it shows the problem is algorithmic rather
than statistical,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.931
and that an exponential moving average of the
weights fixes it,

00:02:38.011 --> 00:02:41.713
even though most standard mitigations fail.

00:02:41.793 --> 00:02:45.494
Third,

00:02:45.574 --> 00:02:49.276
it demonstrates the exact same phenomenon and the
same cure in autoregressive language models,

00:02:49.356 --> 00:02:53.057
and complements everything with a convex theory vignette.

00:02:53.137 --> 00:02:56.839
The upshot is a call to treat iterate averaging as
an essential design choice,

00:02:56.919 --> 00:03:00.620
a stabilizer that belongs alongside your optimizer
and your scheduler.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.875
The approach is first to carefully separate the two
possible causes of the oscillations.

00:03:06.955 --> 00:03:12.086
Is it a statistical problem, meaning not enough
data, or a computational one,

00:03:12.166 --> 00:03:15.334
meaning something about the optimization itself?

00:03:15.414 --> 00:03:20.612
By ablating away the statistical difficulty and
still seeing the oscillations,

00:03:20.692 --> 00:03:24.740
the authors pin the blame on gradient variance
amplification.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.973
Then they test the natural fixes.

00:03:27.053 --> 00:03:30.086
Aggressively decaying the learning rate helps,

00:03:30.166 --> 00:03:35.567
and dramatically increasing the batch size helps,
but both cost a lot of compute.

00:03:35.647 --> 00:03:38.139
The star of the paper is much cheaper.

00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:42.808
You simply keep a running exponential moving
average of your weights,

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:44.364
updating it every step,

00:03:44.444 --> 00:03:50.184
defined by theta-tilde at time t plus one equals
one minus gamma times the old average

00:03:50.264 --> 00:03:52.417
plus gamma times the new iterate.

00:03:52.497 --> 00:03:57.154
A supporting theorem shows that in a simple
deterministic environment,

00:03:57.234 --> 00:04:03.177
a tiny perturbation that barely changes the
imitation loss can blow up the rollout reward

00:04:03.257 --> 00:04:05.410
gap exponentially in the horizon.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.941
The experiments span two very different domains.

00:04:10.021 --> 00:04:14.172
For continuous control, the authors use classic
MuJoCo locomotion tasks,

00:04:14.252 --> 00:04:18.403
with Walker2d as the primary testbed and Hopper,
HalfCheetah, Ant,

00:04:18.483 --> 00:04:22.634
and Humanoid for breadth.

00:04:22.714 --> 00:04:26.865
They clone from strong expert policies over a long
horizon of a thousand steps,

00:04:26.945 --> 00:04:31.095
and they deliberately set the dataset size so that
overfitting is not the issue.

00:04:31.175 --> 00:04:35.326
For language,

00:04:35.406 --> 00:04:39.557
they train two hundred and seventy million
parameter Transformers on TinyStories,

00:04:39.637 --> 00:04:43.788
a small synthetic dataset of simple children's
stories that keeps the setup tractable

00:04:43.868 --> 00:04:48.019
while still exhibiting the phenomenon.

00:04:48.099 --> 00:04:52.250
Reward is averaged over twenty initial conditions
so the oscillations are not just measurement noise.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.757
The headline finding is striking.

00:04:54.837 --> 00:05:01.379
Plotting rollout reward against training step
reveals dramatic swings between consecutive iterates,

00:05:01.459 --> 00:05:05.593
and zooming in shows a jagged, almost fractal
reward landscape,

00:05:05.673 --> 00:05:09.406
all while the behavior cloning loss sits flat and smooth.

00:05:09.486 --> 00:05:13.820
That jaggedness is completely invisible in the
one-step objective.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.563
Now apply the exponential moving average,

00:05:16.643 --> 00:05:22.181
and those oscillations are dramatically damped,
across architectures, dataset sizes,

00:05:22.261 --> 00:05:27.465
and multiple tasks, with no change to the learning
rate schedule or batch size.

00:05:27.545 --> 00:05:31.612
And the same butterfly effect shows up in language
generation,

00:05:31.692 --> 00:05:38.703
where two nearly identical training checkpoints
produce stories that diverge into totally different plots.

00:05:38.783 --> 00:05:44.990
Here too the moving average tames the instability
and even yields the lowest-perplexity model.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:48.984
The ablations are what make the diagnosis convincing.

00:05:49.064 --> 00:05:53.165
The oscillations refuse to go away as you vary
dataset size,

00:05:53.245 --> 00:05:59.089
swap a multilayer perceptron for a Transformer,
scale the model, or add regularizers.

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:02.992
Only genuine variance reduction consistently calms them,

00:06:03.072 --> 00:06:07.941
which is exactly what you would predict if gradient
noise is the cause.

00:06:08.021 --> 00:06:13.795
On the fix side, the moving average works best when
you update it every single step,

00:06:13.875 --> 00:06:19.719
use an initial burn-in period, and anneal the
averaging rate with a polynomial decay.

00:06:19.799 --> 00:06:23.761
Skip the burn-in, or update the average only occasionally,

00:06:23.841 --> 00:06:26.201
and its stabilizing power degrades.

00:06:26.281 --> 00:06:29.546
Learning rate decay and giant batches also work,

00:06:29.626 --> 00:06:33.170
but they cost far more compute for the same benefit.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.615
A few numbers anchor the study.

00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:43.225
The empirical investigation covers two hundred and
seventy-eight distinct interventions in continuous control.

00:06:43.305 --> 00:06:46.961
The rollouts run over a horizon of one thousand steps,

00:06:47.041 --> 00:06:53.188
and the theory shows the error amplification
constant grows exponentially in that horizon.

00:06:53.268 --> 00:06:54.641
On the language side,

00:06:54.721 --> 00:07:01.075
the models are two hundred and seventy million
parameter Transformers trained on TinyStories.

00:07:01.155 --> 00:07:09.239
The key contrast in the theory is that a
perturbation changes the behavior cloning loss by only order H delta squared,

00:07:09.319 --> 00:07:14.635
but can change the rollout reward by order H times
e to the H delta minus one,

00:07:14.715 --> 00:07:15.950
an exponential gap.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.803
The takeaway is simple and actionable.

00:07:18.883 --> 00:07:24.850
Stochastic gradient noise is completely harmless
for the one-step training loss you watch,

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.075
but once your model sits inside a feedback loop,

00:07:28.155 --> 00:07:31.032
that same noise gets amplified into chaotic,

00:07:31.112 --> 00:07:33.786
catastrophic failures over long horizons.

00:07:33.866 --> 00:07:36.944
They call this gradient variance amplification.

00:07:37.024 --> 00:07:39.968
The good news is that the fix is almost free.

00:07:40.048 --> 00:07:44.806
Keep an exponential moving average of your weights,
and you get a cheap,

00:07:44.886 --> 00:07:46.754
broadly effective stabilizer.

00:07:46.834 --> 00:07:51.054
Whether you are training a robot controller or a
language model,

00:07:51.134 --> 00:07:55.421
iterate averaging deserves to be a standard part of
the pipeline,

00:07:55.501 --> 00:07:59.520
right next to your optimizer and your learning rate
schedule.
